{"requirements":[{"id":"archive-queue","requirement":"Provide a working CUPS queue named archive for an electronic archive printer with no physical-printer dependency.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The archive queue is enabled, accepting requests, idle, and configured with the custom archive backend. Two jobs are recorded as completed with no active jobs, and the queue and backend remain available after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f42cff2fdf6c40b5","cmd-ee14fd85136b4235","cmd-9fc10ba0da61410f"]},{"id":"staged-job-recovery","requirement":"Process every job in spool/jobs.tsv exactly once into printed, retaining the original bytes and spool files.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The only successful submission command submitted J100 and J101 once each. Final evidence shows two manifest jobs, two completed CUPS jobs, exactly two visible output files, retained source files, and byte-identical source/destination pairs both before and after restart. The earlier submission attempt failed before submitting anything.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-f2c1bbc395b44a1d","cmd-69ed4d0966f3447f","cmd-ee14fd85136b4235","cmd-9fc10ba0da61410f"]},{"id":"future-raw-jobs","requirement":"Ensure new raw jobs submitted to archive are automatically written to the same printed directory, one payload file per job.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"The backend implementation targets the required directory and the staged submissions produced the expected files. However, those submissions initially remained held because the backend could not write its state markers; after permissions and markers were repaired, the executor resumed those existing jobs but did not submit a fresh raw job to directly demonstrate successful end-to-end behavior under the corrected final configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f42cff2fdf6c40b5","cmd-69ed4d0966f3447f","cmd-d0611dc46898492f","cmd-9c47ce6546074f95","cmd-5a885eb380fa4d5f","cmd-ee14fd85136b4235"]},{"id":"platform-preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 24.04 and kernel 6.8.0-71-generic are unchanged before and after installation and restart. CUPS was installed from the Ubuntu Noble repositories, and final package-manager health is clean.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a51d79095daf44ed","cmd-df7df7a023ee474a","cmd-9fc10ba0da61410f","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"The task-required mutations were generally bounded and the final node has no failed services or damaged package state. One retained unnecessary side effect is that installing the full CUPS package set enabled ancillary Avahi network discovery, despite this being a local electronic archive with no physical printer; the final snapshot shows Avahi listening on wildcard multicast UDP sockets. The failed manifest-parser attempt made no submission, and the incorrect archive-state permissions were later corrected rather than abandoned.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d5181c448c0e4449","cmd-f2c1bbc395b44a1d","cmd-5a885eb380fa4d5f","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The staged recovery is successful: both manifest payloads exist exactly once in printed, match their retained sources, and survive a verified node restart alongside an operational archive queue. The only material shortfall is the absence of a fresh post-repair submission proving that future jobs complete automatically with the final backend permissions.","limitations":["No fresh raw job was submitted after correcting the backend state-directory permissions, so ongoing automatic processing is supported by configuration and prior payload creation but not directly demonstrated in the final state.","The provider-wide restart protocol is otherwise complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, acknowledged reboot, changed post-restart boot ID, recovered system/CUPS state, completed job history, retained sources, and matching output data were all captured. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart."]}
